---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${guide.title} | Fix The Speaker`}
  description={guide.description}
  heading={guide.title}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  updated={guide.updated}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> not really. Rice is a weak drying agent, it can leave dust and grains in your
    charging port, and Apple now tells iPhone owners not to use it. Leaving your phone in a dry spot with moving air
    works at least as well, and usually better.
  </p>

  <h2>Why people put wet phones in rice</h2>
  <p>
    The idea is simple: uncooked rice absorbs moisture, so burying a phone in it should pull water out of the
    phone. It feels like you are doing something useful, and almost everyone has rice in the kitchen.
  </p>
  <p>
    The problem is that most of the water that hurts a phone is <em>inside</em> it: behind the speaker mesh, in the
    charging port, or under the screen. Rice only touches the outside of the phone. It cannot reach into those
    spaces any better than the surrounding air can.
  </p>

  <h2>What testing has shown</h2>
  <p>
    In 2014 the phone reseller Gazelle ran a small drying test. They soaked sponges and phones, then left them in
    rice, several other drying agents, and open air. Plain uncooked rice absorbed the least water of the seven
    materials they tried, and the sponge left in open air dried better than any of the materials. In their phone
    test, open air again did better than sealing the phone in rice.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was a small test run by a company that sells phones, so it is not the final word. But it matches what you
    would expect from the physics, and we have not found any careful test showing rice beats open air.
  </p>

  <h2>What Apple says</h2>
  <p>Apple's support page for the iPhone "Liquid Detected" alert is direct about it:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Don't put your iPhone in a bag of rice. Doing so could allow small particles of rice to damage your iPhone."</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The same page also warns against drying an iPhone with "an external heat source or compressed air", and against
    pushing "a cotton swab or a paper towel" into the connector.
  </p>

  <h2>Why rice can make things worse</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Starch dust and broken grains.</strong> Rice sheds fine powder. It can collect in the charging port,
      the speaker grille, and the microphone holes, which are exactly the openings you want to keep clear.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It blocks airflow.</strong> A phone buried in a bowl of rice sits in still air. Water evaporates faster
      when air is moving across the openings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It makes you wait instead of acting.</strong> The first few minutes matter most. Getting the phone
      unplugged and the excess water out right away does more than any amount of rice later.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to do instead</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Get it out of the water and wipe it dry.</strong> Use a soft, lint-free cloth on the outside.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unplug everything.</strong> Disconnect the charging cable and any accessories. Take off the case, since
      cases trap water against the phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tap out the excess water.</strong> Apple recommends tapping the phone gently against your hand with the
      charging port facing down. Do the same with the speaker openings facing down.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If it was not plain water, rinse it.</strong> For iPhone, Apple's advice after contact with liquids
      other than water is to rinse the affected area with tap water, then wipe it dry. This keeps sticky or salty
      residue from drying inside the openings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leave it somewhere dry with airflow.</strong> Apple says a fan blowing cool air directly into the
      charging port may help. Do not use a hair dryer, an oven, a radiator, or compressed air.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wait before you charge it.</strong> Apple's "Liquid Detected" page says to wait at least 30 minutes
      before trying to charge again, and that full drying can take up to 24 hours. Apple's water resistance page
      suggests allowing at least 5 hours before charging with a cable. If you can wait, wait longer. Wireless
      charging avoids the wet port, but only use it once the back of the phone is dry.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If the speaker still sounds muffled</h2>
  <p>
    A muffled speaker after getting wet is usually water sitting in the speaker grille, held there by surface
    tension. Hold the phone with the speaker facing down and play a low-frequency tone at full volume: the speaker
    cone moves back and forth and pushes droplets out through the mesh. Apple Watch has a similar feature built in.
    Our <a href="/">free water eject tool</a> plays this kind of tone in your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    Be realistic about what this does. It helps clear water from the speaker grille. It does not dry the inside of
    the phone and cannot repair damage that has already happened.
  </p>

  <h2>When to take it to a repair shop</h2>
  <p>Drying at home will not fix every phone. Get it checked by an authorized service provider if:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>it will not turn on, or it restarts on its own</li>
    <li>it gets warm, or the back or screen starts to bulge</li>
    <li>there are spots, lines, or fog under the screen</li>
    <li>the "Liquid Detected" warning keeps coming back after a full day of drying</li>
    <li>the sound is still crackly or distorted after the phone has fully dried</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two more things to keep in mind. Water resistance wears down over time: Apple notes that splash, water, and dust
    resistance "are not permanent conditions". And on iPhone, liquid damage is not covered by Apple's standard
    warranty, so check what your own warranty or insurance covers before paying for a repair.
  </p>

  <h2>Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>Is silica gel better than rice?</h3>
  <p>
    In Gazelle's test, silica gel absorbed more water than rice. But open air still dried the sponge better than
    any of the materials they tried, so a dry room with a fan is a good choice even without silica gel.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I charge my phone in an emergency?</h3>
  <p>
    On iPhone, Apple's "Liquid Detected" page says you can override the alert to charge in an emergency, but you
    should not charge a wet phone if you can avoid it.
  </p>
  <h3>Should I turn the phone off?</h3>
  <p>
    If the phone was fully submerged, turning it off while it dries is a sensible precaution: there is less
    current flowing through any wet parts. Avoid repeatedly turning it on to check whether it works.
  </p>

